# His Research - Our Thanks!
Tamil title: அவருடைய ஆராய்ச்சி - நமது நன்றி!

“The white elephant appears with four ornamental bands. A bearded young man rides upon it, holding cymbals in his hands. To the right of this scene appears a rapidly moving horse, upon which is seated a handsome, well-built man. The elephant and horse walk through water. Waves and fish are clearly visible in the water.”

This is how an artist who lived eight hundred and fifteen years ago used his artistic skill to depict the journey of Sundarar and his friend Cheraman to Kailasa. We must first discuss the research and explanation written by one of our friends concerning how far this event, and the explanation attached to it, are true. The people ought to understand what research means, how it should be conducted, and why a subject is investigated. We therefore consider the research our contemporary friend has conducted into this event.

He writes:

“Sekkizhar says that Sundarar and Cheraman travelled through the sky to Kailasa. By contrast, the mural in the Thanjavur Great Temple clearly depicts a journey by sea. Had it been painted after the *Periyapuranam*, it would have shown an aerial journey. Since it does not, it is reasonable to regard the mural as earlier than the *Periyapuranam*. Sekkizhar accepted that Sundarar rode a white elephant and Cheraman a horse, but he did not accept the mural's indication that they travelled by sea. Why? From the Chera capital, a sea journey could proceed westward, but it could not reach the Himalayas lying upon the northern landmass. Nambi refers in three places to Sundarar and Cheraman going to Kailasa, but does not say whether they went by sea or by air. This omission is noteworthy. Does his failure to state either not clearly reveal his Non-Committal Policy?”

To say that he has “written” this means that he has examined the event and announced his conclusion. What is that conclusion?

In his preface our friend says of Sekkizhar, “No one else in Tamil has composed an epic after undertaking such arduous research.” Anyone who reads this can see that his entire purpose is to place Sekkizhar in the foreground, confer distinction upon him, and reserve excellence for his book. His purpose is not to investigate a particular event, explain the truths obtained from it, and perform useful work for the people.

Why do we say so? In his preface he also writes:

“It is a matter for pleasure to learn that M. Bhaktavatsalam, now one of the ministers of our Presidency, belongs to Sekkizhar's lineage.”

According to his calculation, Sekkizhar lived in CE 1162, seven hundred and eighty-six years ago. Why did our friend create an occasion to rejoice by connecting today's Comrade Bhaktavatsalam with a man who lived 786 years earlier? Only by examining this closely do we see that his purpose could not stop with praising Sekkizhar. He searches for anyone in Sekkizhar's lineage who can be praised, praises that person, and thereby seeks honour and esteem for Sekkizhar. This is an undeniable truth.

Otherwise, why would a researcher entering upon the *Periyapuranam* drag in Comrade Bhaktavatsalam, who has neither connection nor necessity in his research, stand him before us, and say, “Look! He too belongs to Sekkizhar's lineage,” in an effort to elevate Sekkizhar?

Thiru. Vi. Ka., who supplied a commendatory preface to this *Periyapuranam Research*, said that its author had laboured greatly, whirling around until heaven and earth shook, to give form to his conception. In keeping with that statement, the effort our friend has made to elevate Sekkizhar is great, great, alas, very great indeed!

When we reflect that 786 years had to pass before someone within Sekkizhar's lineage could be cited as evidence of his greatness, even we are moved to the pitiable condition of wanting to say something in his praise. But the moral injunction that a Tamil must not take up the base livelihood of singing praise restrains us.

Let us now take up our friend's account and investigation of Sundarar and Cheraman's journey to Kailasa.

He says:

“Sekkizhar accepted that Sundarar travelled upon a white elephant and Cheraman upon a horse. Sekkizhar states that Sundarar and Cheraman travelled through the sky to Kailasa.”

This means that he has researched and reached a firm conclusion. His research thus ends with the claim that an elephant and horse carried Sundarar and Cheraman, flew through the sky, and reached Kailasa.

Surely everyone would wish to know whether this research conclusion accords with reason and nature and can benefit human life. That is the natural question for people living in an age of expanding knowledge. What benefit or enlightenment can the people obtain merely from being told that the Kailasa-entry scene in the Thanjavur Great Temple is correct?

Had our friend examined whether the people could gain understanding and benefit from the events described, his work would have aided intellectual enlightenment and the progressive effort to perceive truths as they are. Instead, he has left people like us the rare labour of researching his research.

If someone called a researcher creates a condition in which a work called research must itself be investigated, it is needless to explain what the scholarly world will think of that person and his conclusions. Yet the friend who published *Periyapuranam Research* is also a Tamil. It is therefore the duty of Tamils like us to take up what he failed to do, whether through error, forgetfulness, ignorance of humanity's condition, or religious intoxication, and explain its truth.

When a Tamil makes a mistake, merely pointing it out and accusing him would itself be contrary to Tamil culture. Correcting such a person does not merely display another person's ability, for ability and inability are not anyone's permanent possession. Continuing the work our friend left undone is a great service freely rendered by one Tamil to another. We therefore trust that he will lovingly welcome this service performed for him, because he knows us well and we know him.

The history stated at the beginning is this: while in Kailasa, Sundarar lusted after two women there, Aninditai and Kamalini. Shiva sent him into this world, where he completed the task for which he had come and then returned to Kailasa.

While Sundarar was staying with his friend Cheraman at Tiruvanjaikkalam in the Malayalam country, Shiva one day summoned his servants, Brahma, Vishnu, the ganas, and their leaders, and ordered: “Take a white elephant from here, go and bring our friend Sundaran to this place.” They departed with the white elephant, reached Tiruvanjaikkalam, seated Sundarar upon it, and set out.

Hearing this, Cheraman uttered the five-letter mantra, the Panchakshara, into a horse's ear. Mounting it, he flew through the sky ahead of Sundarar. Seeing this, Cheraman's bodyguards all flew upward and went ahead of both men. On his way to Kailasa, Sundarar sang a hymn and gave it to Varuna, king of the sea, who brought it back to Tiruvanjaikkalam.

A sage named Upamanyu saw Sundarar flying through the sky upon the white elephant and told his students of Sundarar's greatness and of the devotees whose histories appeared in the *Tiruthondathogai* composed by him. That, in brief, is Sundarar's story. For its expansion and “illuminating research,” see our *Periyapuranam Research*. What we examine here is the *Periyapuranam Research* newly, or rather novelly, written by our friend.

The expression “white elephant” in this tale perhaps describes the colour of elephants in the upper world called Shiva's realm. Just as the Aryans or descendants of Shiva in that world and this one are white, perhaps its other animals and birds are also white! We have never heard that any elephant in this Jambudvipa was or is naturally white. Some elephants may perhaps acquire whiteness as some human beings lose their natural colour through leucoderma, though we have not seen even such elephants. Perhaps elephants do not engage, as humans do, in contracting a skin disease and turning their bodies white!

We need not extend our research into the elephant's colour. But the elephant that came from Kailasa is said to have possessed the power of flight. Perhaps elephants in Kailasa can both walk and fly. Who knows? Only if we had previously visited Kailasa could we determine the truth.

We do not know whether the sage Upamanyu had ever visited Kailasa. He says that while standing at its foot he saw a white elephant carrying Sundarar through the sky. He is not alone; Sekkizhar says the same. Nor does the pitiable support for this assertion stop there. Our friend, who has now researched the *Periyapuranam* “by a new method,” completes his research by accepting without hesitation both that the white elephant existed and that it flew.

Our friend has had the good fortune to live in an age when birds, naturally endowed for flight, move through the sky and human beings, using scientific methods and machines, make vehicles called aeroplanes fly. Yet he cannot perceive the difference between the two. With the maturity of his intellect, he believes what some deluded person wrote about an elephant flying in the sky and spends his brainpower trying to prove it. When we reflect that such a Tamil, a person able to call himself educated, moves among us, we must grieve.

If an elephant, equipped with four legs and the corresponding nature of walking upon them, flew through the sky, the event would be a marvel before which science itself must beg. When madness overtakes some people, however, they may see walking things as flying, stationary things as moving, buildings as swaying, and people standing upon the earth as rising upward. Can we say that such a condition affected Upamanyu, Sekkizhar, or our friend?

Upamanyu and Sekkizhar lived in an age without intellectual enlightenment, when people were taught not to direct their minds toward worldly knowledge. Even if they were not literally delirious, they may have spoken so under a religious intoxication unsuited to humanity. But in this age, with its rare opportunity to understand human character and regulate life's needs accordingly, for our friend to lose his way in shaping his life is to seek an indelible disgrace for his people.

It is said not only that the elephant sent from Kailasa flew for Sundarar, but also that the horse from Cheraman's Malayalam country carried him through the sky ahead of Sundarar. The moment the five letters “Namasivaya” were spoken into its ear, the horse supposedly flew upward like a motor speeding away when petrol is opened and the accelerator pressed.

If elephants, horses, and other animals in this country can fly, why did a white elephant have to come from Kailasa for Sundarar? Could he not, like Cheraman, have spoken those five letters into the ear of some local elephant, horse, dog, or donkey and made it fly? Did Sundarar possess less love for Shiva than Cheraman? Could he not obtain a horse as Cheraman did?

Had Sundarar asked, Cheraman would surely have given him not one but many horses, or even elephants of the Chera country. We are told that they were intimate friends, each prepared to give the other whatever he desired. Nor can it be said that Sundarar originally arrived from Kailasa upon a white elephant and that the customary vehicle was therefore sent for his return.

How did Sundarar come from Kailasa? Did he walk? Did he leap from Shiva's world to the earthly world? The Purana offers no clue. It says only that Sundarar, sent from Kailasa, was born as the son of Sadaiyanar and Isaignaniyar at Tiruvennainallur. Why was he sent secretly, as though disclosure would be shameful, but later summoned back with a white elephant, Brahma, Vishnu, gods, and sages?

What did Sundarar do in Kailasa? He saw two women standing in a flower garden and lusted after them. He and the women were sent here so that he could enjoy that desire. When the work was done, Sundarar was recalled to Kailasa. Omnipotent Shiva arranges this splendid reception for precisely such a man. Behold the excellence of “our” god, who showers grace upon the libertine!

Sekkizhar made a man who adopted illicit conduct contrary to worldly norms the hero of the *Periyapuranam*. On page 277 our friend explains this very well:

“The singing of Sundarar at the end of every canto clearly emphasises that the *Periyapuranam* is a continuous poem. Sundarar's story begins in the Tirumalai, or Kailasa, canto and ends in the White Elephant canto, reinforcing that the *Periyapuranam* is an epic about Sundarar.”

He has thus made clear that the *Periyapuranam* was composed for Sundarar.

So there is a Purana for a man who lusted after every woman he saw and engaged in sexual play! Divine quality supposedly shines within it. The people are expected to read it. Its poet was Sekkizhar, said to possess learning in many disciplines. Our respected Comrade Bhaktavatsalam is said to descend from Sekkizhar.

If Comrade Bhaktavatsalam ever finds the leisure, opportunity, and desire to read this *Periyapuranam* and compare its events, there is no doubt that he will truly feel ashamed to have been born in the lineage of a Sekkizhar who knew nothing to sing but such a treasury of lust.

What else is contained in this *Periyapuranam*? Alas! Murder, robbery, theft, gambling, and every cruelty too shameful for the tongue to pronounce or the mind to contemplate constitute its celebrated events. Above all there is the episode titled “Iyarppakai Nayanar,” a horror Tamil people could not behold even by accident in their dreams.

What would happen if someone approached a Tamil man and said, “Give me your wife”? Iyarppakai, as portrayed in the *Periyapuranam*, does precisely this without hesitation and with a generous heart. Our friend explains him in his research:

“He dared give his wife to Shiva.
He killed the opposing relatives.
He sent his wife away with the devotee.
He stood apart from worldly convention.”

But would a Tamil have committed this degraded act? Would he even have consented to it? Why did Sekkizhar include this episode in the *Periyapuranam*? Even when self-forgetful devotion to God had mounted to one's head, would anyone attempt such an improper deed? We do not know why our friend felt no worthy desire to investigate these questions and tell the people the truth.

Perhaps he thought they were not matters for research. Or perhaps he thought that turning his inquiry in this direction might cost him the M.O.L. degree he hoped to obtain. Of these two explanations, his own preface supplies a note acknowledging that the latter, not the former, prevented genuine research:

“The thought occurred that, if the research book were written in English, it could be submitted for the M.O.L. research degree. Later, in January 1945, I submitted the book to the University of Madras. In July of the same year I received the M.O.L. degree.”

By publishing this happy news, our friend has clarified his purpose in beginning the book. Since methods of research capable of rational enlightenment cannot be expected from a lover of degrees of this kind, it has fallen to us, regarding him as our friend and kinsman, to perform his work.

The whole truth will emerge only if we now examine what is said to have happened after Sundarar and Cheraman reached Kailasa. Everyone in Kailasa welcomed Sundarar upon the white elephant and took him to Shiva. But Cheraman, the Tamil king who travelled with him, found no place there. When he reached Kailasa, Shiva's ganas seized him by the neck and threw him outside.

Likewise Tirunavukkarasar, another Tamil, obtained no place in Kailasa, the supposed abode of liberation. Why did this happen? Why could Cheran and Appar not obtain the “rare blessing” given to Sundarar? Who was Sundarar, and who were Cheran and Navukkarasar? The first was a Brahmin; the latter two were Tamils. Apart from this difference, none can be found among the three. In devotion to Shiva, Cheran and Appar can without doubt be said to have shown more love than Sundarar.

Why, then, did the great being shamelessly described as Shiva, impartial between good and bad, embrace only the lustful Sundarar while excluding Cheraman and Tirunavukkarasar, who regarded service to devotees as virtue? Our friend, who has newly written a *Periyapuranam Research*, may have avoided the question for fear of damaging his aim. But can others who wish to render even a little service to knowledge refrain from considering it?

It is later said that Cheraman too obtained a place in Kailasa because he sang the *Jnanavula*. This resembles military recruiters first examining whether candidates possess sound physiques and admitting them only afterward: Cheraman was tested to see whether he could sing about Shiva and admitted once his ability was known. Otherwise, why write that he was admitted to Kailasa because he sang the *Jnanavula*?

Our friend concludes his research by rejecting that Sundarar's elephant and Cheraman's horse travelled by sea and accepting that they travelled through the sky. Seeking to show that an elephant and horse travelling by sea could not reach the specified place, he writes:

“If they travelled by sea from the Chera capital, they could go westward, but they could not reach the Himalayas on the northern landmass.”

This is entirely true. A person who travels west when he must go north cannot reach his destination. Yet our friend might at least have investigated that the Thanjavur Great Temple mural depicting a sea journey agrees with nature to some extent, whereas Sekkizhar's claim of aerial travel agrees not at all with nature or reason.

An elephant and horse travelling by sea may not be able to walk through it, but they can swim for some distance. An elephant and horse travelling through the sky cannot fly even the shortest distance. A horse may leap ten or twenty feet, but it cannot fly. An elephant cannot do even that much.

If the degree awaiting him stood in the way of offering even this explanation, why should a Tamil surrender knowledge, acquire such a degree, carry it around, and wander the country, whatever honour it may confer? Is obtaining a degree the only way to appease hunger? Are there no others?

Throughout *Periyapuranam Research*, our friend insists that the sources Sekkizhar used are flawless and final. Yet when we see how he treats the sources of other people who contributed to the emergence of the *Periyapuranam*, it is clear that he too employs a Non-Committal Policy.

The foremost source used by Sekkizhar was Nambiyandar Nambi's *Tiruthondar Tiruvandadi*. It mentions Sundarar and Cheraman's journey to Kailasa but does not explain whether they went by sea or sky. Our friend admits this:

“Nambi, who refers in three places to Sundarar and Cheraman going to Kailasa, does not mention whether they travelled by sea or through the sky. This omission is noteworthy. Does his failure to state either not clearly reveal his Non-Committal Policy?”

It is hardly welcome that our friend employs the same Non-Committal Policy as Nambiyandar Nambi. Nambi composed a historical canto for the *Periyapuranam* tradition, whereas our friend's book is called research. A non-committal method must never be used in research. Research means presenting what exists as it exists and explaining openly and clearly the truths that appear to the researcher's mind. Evasion must not come near it.

Having pursued his investigation far enough to establish that someone travelling west cannot reach the north, our friend could have applied the proper method, rejected Sekkizhar's claim that an elephant and horse flew through the sky, placed nature and ordinary reason in the foreground, and completed his work. Perhaps he regarded their aerial journey as “divine” and therefore left it unexamined. In his preface he says:

“This research does not include matters connected with religion or liable to differences of opinion. Investigating them is not the purpose of this undertaking.”

He may therefore have classified the flying elephant and horse as a religious matter liable to disagreement. Yet it is astonishing that this “religious connection” permitted him to insist that a westward traveller cannot reach the north, but did not permit him to deny that elephants and horses flew contrary to nature.

When elephants and horses can fly, even one travelling west can surely reach the north. Believers in divine power will not deny this. Our friend could have recognised it and arranged his research accordingly. No one would regard such inquiry as improperly religious or as introducing disputed religious matters.

We remind him of something else. Nambiyandar Nambi, who did not state whether Sundarar and Cheraman travelled by air or sea, and the Thanjavur painter, who showed them travelling by sea, cannot be declared irreligious. Our friend could therefore have remained neutral and written that Sekkizhar might have followed either of their paths in composing the *Periyapuranam*. His objective would not thereby have been harmed.

He should have written that Sekkizhar might have followed Nambi by saying only that Sundarar and Cheraman reached Kailasa, without mentioning either a sea journey or an aerial one. Or, if our friend trusted inscriptions and murals, he should have accepted as true the Thanjavur Great Temple mural showing them travelling by sea and concluded his research accordingly.

Instead, something absent from the inscriptions and unmentioned by Nambiyandar Nambi was accepted solely because Sekkizhar said it, and made the basis of the research conclusion. We regret this.

Many more matters in our friend's *Periyapuranam Research* deserve investigation. Fearing excessive length, we end here. The book fixes dates and discovers inscriptional evidence for a history that cannot benefit human life in any way. It costs five rupees. We are confident that few will buy it and that even those who do cannot read it without error and understand its meaning.

Our thanks are therefore due to the friend who researched and printed the book in a manner ensuring that it will spread neither among the educated nor among ordinary people in the country.

*(Dravida Nadu, 26 September 1948.)*
